Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a series of ideas in practice, from technology supply or the technological imperative to a strict notion that human destiny is determined by an underlying logic associated with scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: the first is that the technological development itself follows a path that goes beyond cultural or political influence; the latter is the idea that technology, in its in turn, has “effects” in societies that are inherent rather than socially conditioned.
For example, one might say that the car necessarily results in the development of roads.
However, a national road network isn’t economically viable for just a few cars, so new production methods are developed to reduce the cost of having an automobile.
Mass car ownership leads to a higher incidence of accidents on the road, which leads to the invention of new techniques in the health system to heal the wounded bodies.
